# Converting `std::string` to `std::vector`

There are several ways to convert a `std::string` to a `std::vector`, depending on what type of vector you need.

## 1. Convert to `std::vector<char>`

The most common conversion, using the range constructor:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string str = "Hello, World!";
std::vector<char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

If you need a null terminator:
```cpp
std::vector<char> vec(str.c_str(), str.c_str() + str.size() + 1);
```

## 2. Convert to `std::vector<uint8_t>` / `std::vector<unsigned char>`

Useful for binary data:

```cpp
std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<uint8_t> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

## 3. Split a string into `std::vector<std::string>`

Splitting by a delimiter:

```cpp
#include <sstream>

std::vector<std::string> split(const std::string& str, char delim) {
    std::vector<std::string> tokens;
    std::stringstream ss(str);
    std::string token;
    while (std::getline(ss, token, delim)) {
        tokens.push_back(token);
    }
    return tokens;
}

// Usage:
auto words = split("one,two,three", ',');
```

## 4. C++20 Approach with Ranges

```cpp
#include <ranges>
#include <vector>

std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec{str.begin(), str.end()};

// Or with ranges::to (C++23)
auto vec2 = str | std::ranges::to<std::vector>();
```

## 5. Using `std::copy` with `back_inserter`

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iterator>

std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec;
std::copy(str.begin(), str.end(), std::back_inserter(vec));
```

## 6. Using `assign()`

```cpp
std::vector<char> vec;
vec.assign(str.begin(), str.end());
```

**Recommendation:** The range constructor (`std::vector<char> vec(str.begin(), str.end())`) is the cleanest and most efficient approach for most cases.

